    "sentence_translations": [
      "Get Out!",
      "",
      "Paul: Today Little Le said to me, “You get out for me”; does she want me to get out, or her to get out?",
      "",
      "Lili: She wants you to get out.",
      "",
      "Paul: Then is this the same as “You get out”?",
      "Why did she say “You get out for me,” and she looked so angry too.",
      "",
      "Lili: This “You … for me” is used when one is angry; it’s normally seldom used because it’s a bit impolite.",
      "For instance, ”You stop for me,” “You listen for me,” “You get out of here for me”…",
      "",
      "Paul: Ah… I see.",
      "",
      "(At this time, Little Le walked in, saw Paul, and was just about to turn around and leave…)",
      "",
      "Paul: Little Le! I stop for you!",
      "",
      "Lili: Paul! It’s “You stop for me,” not “I stop for you.”",
      "",
      "Paul: Huh? Chinese is so hard!",
